  "id": "DrugBank_DB00514 - MONDO_5709",
  "verbalization": "Dextromethorphan, through its indication for Nasopharyngitis, appears to be linked to the Common Cold via a chain of indications. This relationship is supported by the presence of multiple pathways where Dextromethorphan is associated with Nasopharyngitis, which in turn is linked to the Common Cold through different agents—Bromodiphenhydramine, Hydromorphone, and Guaiacol. These agents may act through distinct mechanisms, yet all contribute to the broader pathophysiology of the Common Cold. The shared involvement of Nasopharyngitis suggests a potential role in the inflammatory response or mucosal defense mechanisms that are implicated in cold symptoms. The evidence indicates that Dextromethorphan may influence the course of the Common Cold through its effects on nasal inflammation, though the exact mechanism remains unclear. The integration of these pathways highlights the importance of Nasopharyngitis as a potential intermediate step in the relationship between Dextromethorphan and the Common Cold.",
